    "content": "CREATIVE WORKFLOWS & GENRE-SPECIFIC EDITING PROMETHEUS KNOWLEDGE BASE 1. GENRE-SPECIFIC EDITING PRINCIPLES 1.1 Narrative / Drama Pacing Philosophy : Invisible editing serves the story. Cuts are motivated by emotion, not action. \u2022 Dialogue Scenes : Use the \"reaction shot rule\" - cut to the listener 1-2 seconds BEFORE the speaker finishes, capturing the emotional response. This creates subtext. \u2022 T ension Building: Extend shot duration as tension rises. A 10-second close-up on a character's eyes before a decision creates more tension than rapid cutting. \u2022 Flashbacks / Memory : Use dissolves (18-24 frames) with slight desaturation (-20%) and lifted blacks (milky shadows) to signal temporal shift. \u2022 Revelation Moments : Hold on the reacting character, not the revealed object. The audience cares about the impact, not the object itself. \u2022 Prometheus Default : For drama, disable auto-cut suggestions. Enable \"emotion detection\" to flag moments where extended holds are recommended. 1.2 Documentary / Interview Pacing Philosophy : Truthful pacing respects real time. Let subjects breathe. \u2022 The Pause Rule : Never cut during a subject's pause shorter than 1.5 seconds. Pauses convey thought, emotion, and authenticity. Cutting too early destroys credibility. \u2022 B-Roll Strategy : Use B-roll to cover cuts in interview, but never cover the most emotional or impactful statements. Let the audience see the subject's face during vulnerability. \u2022",
